═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
2. THE DATA — PER LANGUAGE, PER FILE. NOT MIT.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Everything under languages/*/out/ is DERIVED DATA, and it inherits the licence
of the corpus it was derived from. MIT does not apply to it and never did.

The authoritative record is languages/<lang>/sources.json — machine-readable,
one entry per upstream source, each with an explicit licence, a verification
stamp and a redistribution verdict. languages/<lang>/SOURCES.md is the human
version. `make check` refuses a tree whose declarations are inconsistent, and
`make publish-*` refuses to ship a file whose sources are unverified.

Today, for German:

  out/frequency.txt   CC BY 3.0      derived from Leipzig Corpora Collection
  out/lemmas.tsv      CC BY-SA 4.0   derived from Universal Dependencies

⚠️ Those two do not mix. Bundling a share-alike file into a permissive dataset
makes the whole thing share-alike, so they are published — if at all —
separately. The gate enforces this rather than trusting anyone to remember it.
